<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">August Strindberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Johan August Strindberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">August Strindberg was a Swedish playwright, novelist, poet, essayist and painter, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, who wrote more than 60 plays and more than 30 works of fiction, autobiography, history and politics. His The Red Room (1879) has often been described as the first modern Swedish novel. In The Father (1887), Miss Julie (1888) and Creditors (1889) he wrote naturalistic dramas. After a series of apparent psychotic attacks between 1894 and 1896, his so-called Inferno crisis, he turned to dream plays such as To <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and A Dream Play (1902), a precursor of expressionism and surrealism. From 1907 he helped run the Intimate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in Stockholm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/august-strindberg.jpg" alt="August Strindberg" width="330" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AugustStrindberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Strindberg was born on 22 January 1849 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, the third surviving son of Carl Oscar Strindberg, a shipping agent, and Eleonora Ulrika Norling, a serving-maid. His autobiographical novel The Son of a Servant describes a childhood marked by emotional insecurity, poverty, religious fanaticism and neglect. He spent four years at a harsh school in Klara and then six at the Stockholm Lyceum. His mother died when he was thirteen, and his father married the children's governess the next year. He enrolled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala-university">Uppsala University</a> in September 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Royal Theatre rejected Master Olof in 1872, and its premiere at the New Theatre on 30 December 1881 gave him his breakthrough. The Red Room (1879) had made him known as a novelist. In the late 1880s he wrote the naturalistic dramas The Father, Miss Julie and Creditors, and in 1889 he modelled his short-lived Scandinavian Experimental Theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> on André Antoine's Théâtre Libre. By 1892 he had writer's block and a drastic loss of income, and a fund raised through a German magazine let him leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> for artistic circles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><p>A series of apparent psychotic attacks between 1894 and 1896, his Inferno crisis, led to hospitalisation. He then resolved to become the Zola of the Occult and returned to play-writing in 1898 with To Damascus. From 1907 he helped run the Intimate Theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, which staged chamber plays such as The Ghost Sonata.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He fell ill with pneumonia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1911 and never fully recovered, and stomach cancer brought a painful final few weeks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>'s daily papers reported on his health in every edition. He died on 14 May 1912, aged 63, shortly after The Father opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 9 April 1912.</p><p>Eugene O'Neill devoted much of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> speech to Strindberg's influence, and Tennessee Williams, Edward Albee, John Osborne and Ingmar Bergman have also cited him. Besides plays he painted 117 works now regarded as among the most original of the century. The Strindberg Museum on Drottninggatan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> opened in 1960, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s literary Augustpriset is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “August Strindberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
